# Project AI Coding Guidelines

## Project Context

MVP-focused project prioritizing speed, maintainability, and clean code. Target users are older adults requiring clarity-first UX design.

## Tech Stack

- **UI Framework**: Ant Design (antd)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS (utility classes for spacing, layout adjustments only)
- **Language**: TypeScript (strict mode)

## Code Principles

### 0. Documentation First (CRITICAL)

- **ALWAYS** check `API_DOCS.md` and `db.dbml` before writing any code or answering questions about implementation.
- `API_DOCS.md` is the source of truth for API endpoints, payloads, and response structures.
- `db.dbml` is the source of truth for the database schema and relationships.
- If user specifications conflict with `API_DOCS.md` or `db.dbml`, clarify with the user before proceeding.

### 1. Minimal & DRY

- Write the least amount of code that solves the problem
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks (`use*.ts`)
- Extract reusable UI patterns into components only when used 3+ times
- Prefer composition over abstraction
- No premature optimization

### 2. File Structure

```
src/
  components/     # Shared UI components
  features/       # Feature-based modules (components + hooks + types)
  hooks/          # Shared custom hooks
  utils/          # Pure utility functions
  types/          # Shared TypeScript types
```

### 3. Component Guidelines

- One component per file
- Props interface defined above component
- Destructure props in function signature
- Use Ant Design components as primary building blocks
- Tailwind only for: spacing (`p-*`, `m-*`, `gap-*`), flex/grid layout, responsive adjustments

```tsx
interface UserCardProps {
  name: string;
  email: string;
  onEdit: () => void;
}

export function UserCard({name, email, onEdit}: UserCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card className="mb-4">
      <Typography.Title level={4}>{name}</Typography.Title>
      <Typography.Text>{email}</Typography.Text>
      <Button onClick={onEdit}>Edit</Button>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

### 4. State Management

- Local state: `useState` for simple, `useReducer` for complex
- Server state: React Query / SWR
- No global state unless absolutely necessary
- Lift state only when needed

### 5. TypeScript

- Strict mode enabled
- No `any` type
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for objects
- Use discriminated unions for complex states

## UI/UX: Clarity First Design

Target audience: older adults. Every design decision must prioritize readability and ease of use.

### Visual Hierarchy

- **Font sizes**: Minimum 16px body, 14px secondary text
- **Line height**: Minimum 1.5
- **Contrast**: WCAG AA compliance minimum (4.5:1 for text)
- **Spacing**: Generous whitespace, minimum 16px between interactive elements

### Interactive Elements

- **Button sizes**: Use `size="large"` as default
- **Click targets**: Minimum 44x44px touch area
- **Hover/Focus states**: Clearly visible, high contrast
- **Loading states**: Always show feedback, use Spin or Skeleton

### Forms

- **Labels**: Always visible, above input (no floating labels)
- **Input sizes**: Use `size="large"` as default
- **Error messages**: Inline, red text below field, clear language
- **Help text**: Show before user makes mistakes
- **Validation**: On blur, not on every keystroke

```tsx
<Form.Item
  label="Email Address"
  name="email"
  rules={[
    {required: true, type: "email", message: "Please enter a valid email"},
  ]}
  help="We will send confirmation to this email"
>
  <Input size="large" placeholder="example@email.com" />
</Form.Item>
```

### Navigation & Actions

- **Primary action**: One clear primary button per screen
- **Destructive actions**: Require confirmation, use red color
- **Back/Cancel**: Always provide exit path
- **Progress**: Show steps for multi-page flows

### Feedback & Communication

- **Success/Error**: Use Alert or Message component, clear language
- **Empty states**: Helpful message + action suggestion
- **Loading**: Skeleton for content, Spin for actions

### Ant Design Defaults

```tsx
// ConfigProvider at app root
<ConfigProvider
  theme={{
    token: {
      fontSize: 16,
      borderRadius: 8,
    },
    components: {
      Button: {controlHeight: 44},
      Input: {controlHeight: 44},
      Select: {controlHeight: 44},
    },
  }}
>
  {children}
</ConfigProvider>
```

## Code Patterns

### API Calls

```tsx
async function fetchUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const response = await api.get(`/users/${id}`);
  return response.data;
}

function useUser(id: string) {
  return useQuery(["user", id], () => fetchUser(id));
}
```

### Event Handlers

```tsx
const handleSubmit = async (values: FormValues) => {
  try {
    await createUser(values);
    message.success("User created successfully");
  } catch {
    message.error("Failed to create user. Please try again.");
  }
};
```

### Conditional Rendering

```tsx
{
  isLoading && <Skeleton active />;
}
{
  error && <Alert type="error" message="Failed to load data" />;
}
{
  data && <UserList users={data} />;
}
```

## Do NOT

- Add comments unless logic is non-obvious
- Create abstractions for single-use code
- Use Tailwind for colors, typography, or component styling (use Ant Design)
- Use placeholder text as labels
- Use icon-only buttons without tooltips
- Use small text (< 14px) for important information
- Nest ternary operators
- Create files with < 20 lines of meaningful code (consolidate)

## Do

- Use Ant Design components for all UI elements
- Use Tailwind for layout and spacing adjustments
- Keep components under 150 lines
- Name functions and variables descriptively
- Handle loading, error, and empty states
- Test interactive flows with keyboard navigation
- Provide immediate visual feedback for all actions
